    "# NPTEL Study Notes: Introduction to Large Language Models (LLMs)\n",
    "\n",
    "## Lecture: High-Level Overview of Natural Language Processing (NLP)\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. Introduction to NLP\n",
    "\n",
    "**Natural Language Processing (NLP)** sits at the intersection of **Computer Science (CS)**, **Human Language**, and **Artificial Intelligence (AI)**.\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Natural vs. Artificial Languages:** Natural languages evolve naturally over time before grammar is formalized (e.g., English, Bengali). Artificial languages are defined by strict grammar rules from inception (e.g., Python, C++).\n",
    "* **Linguistic Diversity:** NLP is incredibly complex due to the sheer volume of languages—over 6,000 official languages globally, and an estimated 19,000 languages/dialects in India alone.\n",
    "* **Pioneers:**\n",
    "* **Alan Turing:** Considered the father of NLP/AI. Proposed the **Turing Test** (Computing Machinery and Intelligence) to evaluate if a machine can exhibit human-like intelligence in conversation.\n",
    "* **Noam Chomsky:** The father of modern linguistics (proposed Universal Grammar and Chomsky Normal Form).\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Why is NLP so Challenging?\n",
    "\n",
    "NLP systems struggle because human language is fundamentally ambiguous, context-dependent, and constantly evolving.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Key Challenges (Exam Focus)\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Ambiguity:** The surface meaning (literal text) often differs from the actual semantic meaning.\n",
    "* *Lexical Ambiguity:* \"Donald Trump's death\" vs. \"Trump card\" (Noun vs. Verb). \"Mary had a little lamb\" (Owning a pet vs. eating meat).\n",
    "* *Syntactic Ambiguity:* \"I saw a girl with a telescope.\" (Did I use the telescope to see her, or was she holding the telescope?). Same syntax, two valid parse trees.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Pragmatics & Context:** The meaning relies heavily on tone, context, and intent (e.g., Hindi phrase: *Mujhe apko mithai khilani paregi* – who is treating whom?).\n",
    "3. **Punctuation Sensitivity:** Minor punctuation changes alter entirely what a sentence means:\n",
    "* *Example 1:* \"Let's eat grandma\" vs. \"Let's eat, grandma.\"\n",
    "* *Example 2:* \"A woman without her man is nothing\" vs. \"A woman: without her, man is nothing.\"\n",
    "* *Example 3:* \"Buffalo buffalo Buffalo buffalo buffalo buffalo Buffalo buffalo.\" (Valid sentence exploiting Buffalo as an animal, a city, and a verb meaning 'to bully').\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "4. **Non-Standard Text:** Social media introduces Out-Of-Vocabulary (OOV) words like *LOL*, *retweet*, and *unfriend*.\n",
    "5. **Multi-word Expressions / Idioms:** Words whose combined meaning is completely different from their individual meanings (e.g., \"Hot dog\" is food, not a warm canine).\n",
    "6. **Segmentation Issues:** Simple space-delimiting fails for tokens like URLs, decimal numbers, hashtags (`#greatdayever`), or entities with internal spaces (\"New York\").\n",
    "7. **World Knowledge:** Models lack common sense. Early Stanford parsers misidentified *Michelle Obama* (*Transcription corrected from \"Mitchell\"*).\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. The NLP Pipeline: Layers of Processing\n",
    "\n",
    "NLP moves from bottom (surface text) to top (abstract meaning), increasing in computational complexity. NLP is broadly divided into **Natural Language Understanding (NLU)** and **Natural Language Generation (NLG)**.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Layer 1: Tokenization\n",
    "\n",
    "Splitting text into meaningful units (tokens). As discussed, simple space-based tokenization fails for URLs, hashtags, and numbers.\n",
    "\n",
    "* *Modern LLM Tokenizers (Transcription Corrections applied):* **Byte Pair Encoding (BPE)**, **WordPiece**, and **SentencePiece**.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Layer 2: Morphology (Word Level)\n",
    "\n",
    "The study of word structure, including stems, roots, prefixes, and suffixes.\n",]
"\n",
    "* *Language Variance:* English is morphologically poor, while Hindi and Hungarian are morphologically rich (e.g., verbs change based on gender in Hindi). *(Note: The instructor flagged the internal mechanisms of Hindi gender-based morphology as personally difficult to fully parse).*\n",
    "* **Stemming vs. Lemmatization (High-Yield for Exams):**\n",
    "| Feature | Stemming | Lemmatization |\n",
    "| --- | --- | --- |\n",
    "| **Output** | Returns a \"stem\" which **may not** be a valid dictionary word. | Returns a \"lemma\" which is **always** a valid dictionary word. |\n",
    "| **Speed** | Very fast (uses heuristic truncation rules). | Slower (requires a dictionary lookup). |\n",
    "| **Use Case** | Good when speed is critical and mapping two words to the same root is enough. | Good when linguistic accuracy is required. |\n",
    "\n",    "\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Layer 3: Syntax (Sentence Level)\n",
    "\n",
    "Concerns how words combine to form valid grammatical sentences.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **POS Tagging (Part of Speech):** Assigning tags (Noun, Verb, Adjective).\n",
    "* *Standard Tagsets:* Penn Treebank (45 tags), C7 tagset (146 tags).\n",
    "* *Context is required:* \"He went to the **park**\" (Noun) vs. \"He went to **park** the car\" (Verb).\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Chunking:** Grouping POS-tagged words into functional phrases (Noun Phrases (NP), Verb Phrases (VP), Prepositional Phrases (PP)).\n",
    "3. **Parsing:** Building a syntactic tree to identify the grammatical structure of the sentence and resolve syntactic ambiguities.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Layer 4: Semantics (Meaning Level)\n",
    "\n",
    "Extracting the actual meaning of words and how they combine. There are three primary approaches to modeling semantics:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Decompositional Semantics:**\n",
    "* Breaks words down into binary feature vectors.\n",
    "* *Example:* Boy = [+Human, -Female, -Adult]. Woman = [+Human, +Female, +Adult].\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Ontological Semantics:**\n",
    "* Uses predefined dictionaries/graphs (e.g., **WordNet**).\n",
    "* Maps relations between entities: *Synonyms*, *Antonyms*, *Hyponyms* (is-a), *Meronyms* (has-part).\n",
    "* *Drawback:* Requires intense manual labor and struggles to keep up with new/evolving vocabulary.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "3. **Distributional Semantics (Foundation of Modern Deep Learning):**\n",
    "* *Principle:* \"A word is characterized by the company it keeps\" (its surrounding context words).\n",
    "* **Co-occurrence Matrix:** A square matrix mapping unique tokens against unique tokens. Values represent how often words co-appear within a specific sliding window.\n",
    "* *Vectorization:* Each row acts as a vector for that token. Similarity between words (e.g., \"Dog\" and \"Cat\") is calculated mathematically using measures like **Cosine Similarity** or dot product.\n",
    "* *Note:* This is the fundamental premise behind algorithms like **Word2Vec** and **GloVe**.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Layer 5: Pragmatics and Discourse (Context Level)\n",
    "\n",
    "The hardest tier of NLP.\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Pragmatics:** The negotiation of meaning between speaker and listener. Extracting intent beyond literal meaning. (e.g., \"Do you have the time?\" means \"Tell me the time,\" not a literal yes/no question. \"Can you pass the water?\" requires the action of passing it, not just saying \"Yes, I can.\")\n",
    "* **Discourse:** Understanding context over a multi-turn dialogue (e.g., tracking subjects across multiple sentences in a chatbot session).\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4. The \"NLP Trinity\"\n",
    "\n",
    "NLP can be visualized as a 3D space intersecting:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Algorithms:** HMM (Hidden Markov Models), CRF (Conditional Random Fields), MEMM, Deep Neural Networks, LLMs.\n",
    "2. **Tasks:** Parsing, POS tagging, Machine Translation, etc.\n",
    "3. **Languages:** English, Hindi, French, etc.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5. Standard NLP Evaluation Tasks\n",
    "\n",
    "To measure model capability, we use these distinct standard tasks:\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Semantic Role Labeling (SRL):** Assigning roles like *Agent* (doer), *Patient* (receiver), *Source*, and *Destination* to words in a sentence.\n",
    "* **Textual Entailment:** Determining if a second sentence logically follows from the first.\n",
    "* *Positive Entailment:* S1: \"Ram hit Shyam\", S2: \"Shyam got hurt.\"\n",
    "* *Negative Entailment:* S1: \"Ram hit Shyam\", S2: \"Shyam did not get hurt.\"\n",
    "* *Non-Entailment:* S1: \"Ram hit Shyam\", S2: \"Shyam scored a goal.\" (Unrelated).\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Coreference Resolution:** Identifying what pronouns/noun phrases refer to in a larger text (e.g., \"John showed Bob his car. **He** liked **it**.\" -> Does *He* refer to John or Bob?).\n",
    "* **Information Extraction (IE):** Pulling structured data from unstructured text. Includes **Named Entity Recognition (NER)** (identifying Person, Location, Organization tags) and **Relation Extraction**.\n",
    "* **Word Sense Disambiguation (WSD):** Determining which dictionary meaning of a word is used (e.g., \"Apple\" as a fruit vs. \"Apple\" as a company).\n",
    "* **Machine Translation (MT):** Translating text between languages. An incredibly old problem (since 1965) that remains unsolved due to inherent linguistic biases and literal translation errors.\n",
    "* **Text Summarization:**\n",
    "* *Extractive:* Identifies important sentences and copy-pastes them verbatim.\n",
    "* *Abstractive:* Understands the core meaning and generates newly phrased sentences (closer to human summarization).\n",
    "* *Aspect-based:* Summarizes only information related to a specific feature (e.g., summarizing only the \"battery life\" mentions in phone reviews).\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Question Answering:** Factoid (Who is the president?), List (Name the islands), and Descriptive (Explain how X works).\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 6. Practice Questions & Explanations\n",
    "\n",
    "**Q1. In the NLP pipeline, an algorithm correctly identifies that the phrase \"Can you pass the salt?\" is a request to physically move the salt, rather than a question about the listener's physical abilities. Which layer of NLP is responsible for this understanding?**\n",
    "a) Syntax\n",
    "b) Morphology\n",
    "c) Decompositional Semantics\n",
    "d) Pragmatics\n",
    "**Answer:** **d) Pragmatics**\n",
    "*Explanation:* Pragmatics deals with the contextual \"negotiation of meaning between speaker and listener\" and intent beyond the literal text. Syntax handles grammar, and morphology handles word structure.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Q2. Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding Stemming and Lemmatization?**\n",
    "a) Lemmatization is always faster than Stemming because it uses simple truncation rules.\n",
    "b) Stemming guarantees that the output word will be found in a standard dictionary.\n",
    "c) Lemmatization requires access to a vocabulary/dictionary, while Stemming relies on heuristic rules.\n",
    "d) Both processes always output the exact same root string.\n",
    "**Answer:** **c) Lemmatization requires access to a vocabulary/dictionary, while Stemming relies on heuristic rules.**\n",
    "*Explanation:* Stemming is faster but cuts words off crudely, often leaving non-dictionary words. Lemmatization is slower because it references a dictionary to ensure the resulting lemma is a real word.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Q3. If a semantic model identifies that the words \"doctor\" and \"hospital\" are related because they frequently co-occur in the same paragraphs within a large text corpus, which semantic approach is being used?**\n",
    "a) Ontological Semantics\n",
    "b) Distributional Semantics\n",
    "c) Decompositional Semantics\n",
    "d) Word Sense Disambiguation\n",
    "**Answer:** **b) Distributional Semantics**\n",
    "*Explanation:* Distributional semantics relies on the premise that words are defined by their surrounding words in large corpora, often utilizing a co-occurrence matrix. Ontological uses hard-coded dictionaries (like WordNet), and Decompositional uses binary feature vectors.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Q4. Given the two sentences: Sentence A: \"The temperature dropped to minus twenty degrees.\" Sentence B: \"It was a hot summer day.\" How would an NLP textual entailment task classify Sentence B relative to Sentence A?**\n",
    "a) Positive Entailment\n",
    "b) Negative Entailment\n",
    "c) Non-Entailment\n",
    "d) Coreference Entailment\n",
    "**Answer:** **b) Negative Entailment**\n",
    "*Explanation:* Sentence B directly contradicts the premise established in Sentence A.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Q5. What is the primary difference between Extractive and Abstractive text summarization?**\n",
    "a) Extractive summaries are only used for numerical data, while abstractive summaries are used for text.\n",
    "b) Extractive summarization copies exact sentences from the source document, whereas abstractive generates new, rephrased sentences.\n",
    "c) Extractive summarization relies on aspect-based parsing, while abstractive relies on semantic role labeling.\n",
    "d) There is no difference; they are synonyms in NLP.\n",
    "**Answer:** **b)**\n",
    "*Explanation:* As defined in the lecture, extractive just copy-pastes key sentences, while abstractive understands the semantics and generates a fresh summary.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 7. Quick Revision Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "* **NLP Goal:** Bridge human language, AI, and computing. High ambiguity makes this exceptionally hard.\n",
    "* **Ambiguity Types:** Lexical (word meaning), Syntactic (grammar structure), Pragmatic (context/intent).\n",
    "* **Morphology Basics:** Stemming (fast, non-dictionary roots) vs. Lemmatization (slower, valid dictionary words).\n",
    "* **Syntax Hierarchy:** POS Tagging (word tags) $\\rightarrow$ Chunking (phrases) $\\rightarrow$ Parsing (sentence trees).\n",
    "* **Semantics:** Focus heavily on **Distributional Semantics** (co-occurrence matrix, basis for modern embeddings) over older Decompositional and Ontological (WordNet) methods.\n",
    "* **Pragmatics/Discourse:** Understanding context and speaker intent; the hardest part of conversational AI.\n",
    "* **Core Evaluation Tasks:** Know the exact definitions of *Textual Entailment* (Pos/Neg/Non), *Coreference Resolution* (identifying what pronouns map to), *WSD* (distinguishing dictionary meanings), and *Extractive vs. Abstractive Summarization*."
   ]
